<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">How redaction works</h3>

<p class="govuk-body">Contact Lens is configured at the Connect instance level with <code>Attributes.Redaction.Enabled: true</code> and <code>RedactionOutput: REDACTED_AND_ORIGINAL</code>. Both segments are emitted to Kinesis. The consumer Lambda <strong>reads only <code>RedactedSegment</code> and writes only that</strong> to DynamoDB. <code>OriginalSegment</code> is ignored entirely.</p>

<p class="govuk-body">Contact Lens emits placeholders in <code>[NAME_1]</code>, <code>[ADDRESS_2]</code> form. The consumer Lambda transforms these to the documented <code>{NAME}</code>, <code>{ADDRESS}</code> form via a regex <code>r"\[([A-Z_]+)_\d+\]"</code> → <code>"{$1}"</code>, so the SPA renders consistent placeholder shapes regardless of how many distinct entities Contact Lens detects in a single segment.</p>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">What to inspect</h3>

<ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number govuk-list--spaced">
  <li>Open the companion SPA, add <code>?step=5</code> to the URL.</li>
  <li>Scroll the left pane. Each segment has a sentiment chip, green for neutral, amber for moderate distress, red for high distress.</li>
  <li>Click any segment. The PII tokens (your name, your address, your phone) appear as monospace placeholders. There is no fallback path that exposes the original.</li>
  <li>Open the AWS console, find the <code>ContactLensCache</code> DynamoDB table, browse a few items. You will not find any of your real personal details verbatim. The PII residue test (<code>tests/pii-residue.test.mjs</code>) asserts this in CI.</li>
</ol>

<p class="govuk-body">If your call in Step 2 sustained distress for more than two minutes, the Connect Case in the middle pane shows <code>safeguarding_flag: true</code> with a red badge. In a real council deployment this would route the case to a safeguarding-trained agent.</p>
